            "text": "At dawn, the city briefly belongs to no one. Delivery trucks murmur at curbs, traffic lights rehearse their colors for empty intersections, and windows hold the pale reflection of a sky still deciding what kind of day to become. In that quiet interval, ordinary things seem unusually precise: a broom leaning against brick, steam rising from a bakery vent, one pigeon inspecting the platform like a stern conductor.\n\nI like this hour because it reveals how much of life is preparation. The baker has been working for hours. A nurse is ending a shift while someone else ties an apron. Newspapers wait in bundles. Coffee machines warm their metal throats. Nothing dramatic happens, yet thousands of small intentions begin aligning themselves.\n\nBy midmorning, noise will cover the evidence. Doors will slam, phones will ring, and people will hurry beneath appointments, ambitions, and private weather. But the early city offers a gentler lesson: most transformations do not arrive with trumpets. They accumulate through repeated gestures, performed when nobody is watching.\n\nPerhaps that is why beginnings feel hopeful. They do not demand perfection, only participation. Light reaches the upper floors first, then slips downward, touching fire escapes, shop signs, bicycle seats and puddles left by last night's rain. Soon the streets are fully awake. Still, beneath the engines and footsteps, that first silence remains, not erased but carried forward, a hidden foundation. Every crowded afternoon is built upon a moment when the world was spacious, unfinished, and waiting for us to begin."
